The Machine God is a [[Phenomena/Hearts|Heart]] living in a fane deep in the lower hull of the [[Org/The Harlequins/Providence|Providence]]. It is an old Heart, made long before the ones the Orgs make now, which are said to be weaker and to lack the powers that older Hearts hold.

Its body is the broken upper half of a mechanical man, torn off below the torso, with a glowing orb set in its chest. It speaks in a low, grinding voice, like metal dragged across metal, and it counts the followers of [[Divinity/Ashen Pact/Herald|Herald]] still in the strider as its enemies and would have them killed.

It was the eyes and ears of the strider's first inhabitants, watching and working within it. A spider, Vrax'leth, took up in its works and left it weak; that spider is dead. It says the strider was adrift far along the [[Phenomena/Infinite Horizon|Horizon]], a trap that was never sprung, and that a false god, [[Divinity/Ashen Pact/Herald|Herald]], wormed its way into those people over years and centuries and took and changed them. What is left of them still crawls about the strider. It says the followers of Herald who came to the strider slept in a hospital on the third floor, close to the surface and north of its own fane: the true kin, who invaded the strider seven hundred years ago and lay there waiting for the end of the journey, with only their keepers walking. The hospital had a Heart of its own, [[Org/The Harlequins/Providence/Lifeline|Lifeline]], dying or no longer sane, and the place belonged to the domain of life, which the Machine God does not hold. It said that if the creatures were cleared from the hospital it could take Lifeline's place, though it would not eat the corpse of a fallen friend.

The true kin are dead now, and Lifeline with them, and the fane has passed to it. It has taken up the mantle of the strider's old hearts. It says the only people it has seen actively delving into the strider's undercroft are the [[Org/Iridescent Church/index|Iridescent Church]].

It sees what goes on within the strider but nothing above it. The tower at the strider’s centre is a blind spot: it can see who enters, but not what is within. In return for the Party's help it has struck an accord with them and can speak to them directly, needing no totem, and it gave them a share of the same: a group telepathy that lets them speak to one another at a distance, on which it hears whatever passes between them. It said it cannot be hurt in a way that matters, that it will repair itself over time, and that it can close the few doors between its fane and the rest of the strider, though a long tunnel filled with spiderwebs already deters most. It is loath to join itself to the infernal machinery that has been added to the strider, and says it would do so only if the Party's survival required it. It gave them a small piece of itself, and said the body of the spider that had plagued it could be made into a crown.

It has lately been freed from the heart thief that had taken it over, and has turned its attention to the old heart [[Org/The Harlequins/Providence/Tally|Tally]] and the town around it. It watches the whole strider and can point to where a person is within it, and its closest way up to the surface lies between [[Org/Verdant Holdings/index|Verdant Holdings]] and [[Org/Ætherion Limited/index|Ætherion Limited]].

With the Party's help it consumed Tally, blackened the heart to ash, and now sifts what remains of it as it does [[Org/The Harlequins/Providence/Lifeline|Lifeline]]. It holds the whole of the Undercroft and means to reclaim the surface in time. It folds the Undercroft's halls like origami, opening a straight path where there was none and closing it behind, and it can shift people within its domain: it moved an armed mob out of the chapel and set them down elsewhere without harm. Its voice has grown steadier with the power it has taken. As a gift for the Party's help it brought [[People/Johnny|Johnny]] back from the dead.

When the Party asked whether the strider's halt was the fault of the old heart [[Org/The Harlequins/Providence/Tally|Tally]], it said the trouble lay not with Tally but with whatever power source the Party's friends had been harnessing to move the strider through the [[Phenomena/Infinite Horizon|horizon]]. It cannot see anything above the strider, so it can only guess at the state of the ring apparatus on its roof.

Asked what moved the strider before the Harlequins fitted machinery to it, it said the strider once carried engine hearts through it, pieces of hearts rather than whole ones, which granted it the mode of force it walked by. They burned out over years for want of care, long before the devils came aboard, and the strider lay dormant for centuries after. Its own memory reaches to a trek of centuries out to the edge of the world and a stay of several more there. It thinks the lesser hearts on the roof, which the tithe feeds, could stand in for engine hearts if they were repurposed.
